1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,344
The release manager looks at the deployment
history in the afternoon and finds two

2
00:00:09,384 --> 00:00:13,990
deployments, not three. A deployment is
one delivery of the software to a live

3
00:00:14,030 --> 00:00:18,279
system, and here each merge to main
starts a run of the deploy workflow.

4
00:00:18,319 --> 00:00:23,163
The second merge was never deployed on its
own. Nobody cancelled anything by hand.

5
00:00:23,503 --> 00:00:28,595
Was a change lost? Is the pipeline broken?
Before you answer, you need to know what

6
00:00:28,635 --> 00:00:33,480
a concurrency group does by default with
a run that is waiting when another one

7
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:38,674
arrives. The answer is one sentence in the
workflow syntax reference, and most teams

8
00:00:38,714 --> 00:00:41,890
have never read it. Keep
those three merges in mind.

9
00:00:41,930 --> 00:00:43,498
They come back, as trains.

10
00:00:45,338 --> 00:00:49,152
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:49,192 --> 00:00:54,082
A workflow is a file that tells GitHub
Actions which jobs to run when an event

12
00:00:54,122 --> 00:00:59,390
happens. A job is a list of steps on one
fresh machine, the runner, and a run is one

13
00:00:59,430 --> 00:01:00,907
execution of a workflow.

14
00:01:01,247 --> 00:01:05,838
In the last video the production job had
a block you were asked to accept for the

15
00:01:05,878 --> 00:01:10,184
moment: a concurrency group named
production with cancel-in-progress: false.

16
00:01:10,224 --> 00:01:14,815
This video explains it, and then turns to
a different question that every growing

17
00:01:14,855 --> 00:01:19,618
organization meets: how do forty repositories
share one deployment procedure without

18
00:01:19,658 --> 00:01:20,933
copying it forty times?

19
00:01:21,273 --> 00:01:26,388
Two topics. Concurrency groups: one name,
at most one run at a time, and a rule for

20
00:01:26,428 --> 00:01:31,606
everyone else. And reuse: reusable workflows,
composite actions and Docker container

21
00:01:31,646 --> 00:01:36,512
actions, which differ in what they replace.
The reusable workflow gets the most

22
00:01:36,552 --> 00:01:41,543
time, because it's the one that changes
the names of your required checks and the

23
00:01:41,583 --> 00:01:46,761
path of your secrets. A required check is
a result that a rule says must pass before

24
00:01:46,801 --> 00:01:51,233
a merge, and a secret is an encrypted
value stored in GitHub's settings.

25
00:01:51,573 --> 00:01:56,039
The local demonstration is short and makes
one point with Git: which version of a

26
00:01:56,079 --> 00:01:57,819
reusable workflow a caller runs.

27
00:01:59,659 --> 00:02:01,237
Here's what you'll walk away with.

28
00:02:01,577 --> 00:02:02,989
After this video you can:

29
00:02:03,329 --> 00:02:07,669
predict which of several queued runs
deploy under a concurrency group, with and

30
00:02:07,709 --> 00:02:08,833
without cancellation;

31
00:02:09,173 --> 00:02:13,584
choose between a reusable workflow, a
composite action and a container action;

32
00:02:13,924 --> 00:02:17,337
explain what each choice does to
the names of required checks;

33
00:02:17,677 --> 00:02:22,564
say why a caller cannot pass an environment
secret to a reusable workflow and where

34
00:02:22,604 --> 00:02:23,513
it must be read;

35
00:02:23,853 --> 00:02:27,084
explain which version of a
reusable workflow a caller runs.

36
00:02:28,924 --> 00:02:32,602
Concurrency. In one sentence:
a concurrency group is a name.

37
00:02:32,642 --> 00:02:37,807
GitHub lets at most one run or job with that
name execute at a time and decides what

38
00:02:37,847 --> 00:02:39,170
happens to the others.

39
00:02:39,510 --> 00:02:41,767
Precisely, from the workflow syntax.

40
00:02:42,107 --> 00:02:45,565
concurrency can be set for the
whole workflow or for one job.

41
00:02:45,605 --> 00:02:50,209
Its value is a group name, or a mapping
with group, cancel-in-progress and queue.

42
00:02:50,549 --> 00:02:55,416
The default behavior: when a run is queued
and another run in the same group is in

43
00:02:55,456 --> 00:03:00,083
progress, the new one is pending, and
any run already pending in that group is

44
00:03:00,123 --> 00:03:03,433
cancelled. So by default at
most one runs and one waits.

45
00:03:03,773 --> 00:03:09,419
Quick quiz. Three runs arrive in one
group, one after another, with the default

46
00:03:09,459 --> 00:03:13,449
settings. How many of them
finish? A, all three. B, two.

47
00:03:13,489 --> 00:03:15,896
C, only the last one. Your answer?

48
00:03:20,736 --> 00:03:25,463
B, two. The first is in progress and
finishes. The second is pending, and it's

49
00:03:25,503 --> 00:03:29,557
cancelled when the third is queued.
The third waits, and then runs.

50
00:03:29,597 --> 00:03:31,880
If you said A, you're in good company.

51
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:36,056
cancel-in-progress: true also
cancels the run in progress.

52
00:03:36,096 --> 00:03:37,660
It may be an expression.

53
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,829
queue: max, since the seventh of May
2026, keeps up to 100 pending runs and

54
00:03:42,869 --> 00:03:48,218
processes them in order. Combining it with
cancel-in-progress: true is a validation

55
00:03:48,258 --> 00:03:48,607
error.

56
00:03:48,947 --> 00:03:54,331
The group expression may use only the
github, inputs and vars contexts at workflow

57
00:03:54,371 --> 00:03:58,035
level. A context is a named
object of facts about a run.

58
00:03:58,075 --> 00:04:01,872
A job-level group may also use
needs, strategy and matrix.

59
00:04:02,212 --> 00:04:06,688
Group names are case-insensitive, and a
group isn't private to a workflow: two

60
00:04:06,728 --> 00:04:09,989
workflows that use the group
name deploy share one track.

61
00:04:10,329 --> 00:04:13,459
And the documentation states
that ordering isn't guaranteed.

62
00:04:13,799 --> 00:04:17,215
Two patterns cover most needs,
and they want opposite settings.

63
00:04:21,555 --> 00:04:26,452
The first includes the workflow name and
the ref, so that only runs of the same

64
00:04:26,492 --> 00:04:31,701
workflow on the same ref compete. The second
is deliberately the same for every ref.

65
00:04:31,741 --> 00:04:35,138
Workflow 8 uses the second
form for the whole workflow.

66
00:04:35,178 --> 00:04:40,387
Workflow 9 uses one group per environment
at job level, so that a staging deployment

67
00:04:40,427 --> 00:04:42,824
doesn't wait for a production approval.

68
00:04:43,164 --> 00:04:46,627
Why never cancel-in-progress:
true on a deployment?

69
00:04:46,667 --> 00:04:52,053
Section 20B.17: a deployment cancelled
half-way leaves a state nobody designed.

70
00:04:52,393 --> 00:04:56,469
One more sentence from the documentation
that people assume the opposite of:

71
00:04:56,509 --> 00:05:00,693
concurrency and environment aren't
connected. An environment doesn't serialise

72
00:05:00,733 --> 00:05:01,722
anything by itself.

73
00:05:02,062 --> 00:05:06,945
Reuse. Three mechanisms remove duplication.
They differ in what they replace.

74
00:05:07,285 --> 00:05:10,676
A reusable workflow replaces
one or more whole jobs.

75
00:05:10,716 --> 00:05:15,625
It's a workflow file with on: workflow_call,
called with uses at job level.

76
00:05:15,665 --> 00:05:19,254
It chooses its own runners.
It can name an environment.

77
00:05:19,294 --> 00:05:24,203
Its secrets are declared in its interface,
or passed with secrets: inherit.

78
00:05:24,243 --> 00:05:26,644
In the log it shows as separate jobs.

79
00:05:26,984 --> 00:05:30,492
A composite action replaces
several steps inside a job.

80
00:05:30,532 --> 00:05:35,653
It's an action.yml with runs.using:
"composite", called with uses at step level.

81
00:05:35,693 --> 00:05:39,588
It runs on the caller's runner.
It can't name an environment.

82
00:05:39,628 --> 00:05:43,845
It can't read the secrets context,
so values are passed as inputs.

83
00:05:43,885 --> 00:05:45,458
In the log it's one step.

84
00:05:45,798 --> 00:05:50,639
A Docker container action replaces one
step. It's an action.yml with runs.using:

85
00:05:50,679 --> 00:05:55,703
"docker". It needs a Linux runner, and the
documentation notes it's slower than the

86
00:05:55,743 --> 00:05:59,120
other kinds, because the image
is built or pulled first.

87
00:05:59,460 --> 00:06:04,305
Reusable workflows, in one sentence: a
reusable workflow is a workflow file that

88
00:06:04,345 --> 00:06:07,358
another workflow calls as
if it were a single job.

89
00:06:07,698 --> 00:06:12,987
The called file declares its interface under
on.workflow_call: two inputs, each with

90
00:06:13,027 --> 00:06:16,856
a type, one secret, and one
output, mapped from a job output.

91
00:06:17,196 --> 00:06:21,938
The caller has a job named staging whose
body is uses, with and secrets, and a

92
00:06:21,978 --> 00:06:25,065
second job that needs staging
and reads its output.

93
00:06:25,405 --> 00:06:25,956
The rules.

94
00:06:26,296 --> 00:06:30,749
Inputs have a type of boolean, number or
string. Outputs are declared at workflow

95
00:06:30,789 --> 00:06:35,353
level and mapped from job outputs. The
caller reads them through needs and the name

96
00:06:35,393 --> 00:06:36,407
of the calling job.

97
00:06:36,747 --> 00:06:41,870
A calling job isn't a normal job. It may
contain only a fixed set of keys, among

98
00:06:41,910 --> 00:06:47,098
them name, uses, with, secrets, strategy,
needs, if, concurrency and permissions.

99
00:06:47,138 --> 00:06:52,003
It has no runs-on and no steps. No
context or expression is allowed in uses.

100
00:06:52,343 --> 00:06:57,354
Where the file comes from. A path starting
with ./ is the same repository at the

101
00:06:57,394 --> 00:07:02,658
commit of the run. The form with owner,
repository, path and a ref after the at sign

102
00:07:02,698 --> 00:07:07,899
is another repository. Pin the ref to a
full commit ID, for the reason every action

103
00:07:07,939 --> 00:07:08,530
is pinned.

104
00:07:08,870 --> 00:07:15,125
Since the thirtieth of July 2026 the
documentation recommends a form starting with

105
00:07:15,165 --> 00:07:21,112
$/ for the same repository on github.com.
It needs runner 2.336.0 or later and

106
00:07:21,152 --> 00:07:26,716
doesn't exist on GitHub Enterprise
Server. And files in subdirectories of

107
00:07:26,756 --> 00:07:29,326
.github/workflows can't be called.

108
00:07:29,666 --> 00:07:34,400
Secrets pass only one level: a workflow
called by a called workflow needs them

109
00:07:34,440 --> 00:07:39,420
passed again. secrets: inherit passes all
of the caller's secrets and works within

110
00:07:39,460 --> 00:07:44,195
one organization or enterprise. And
environment secrets can't be passed by the

111
00:07:44,235 --> 00:07:49,153
caller, because, in the documentation's
words, "on.workflow_call does not support

112
00:07:49,193 --> 00:07:53,010
the environment keyword". The
called job names the environment.

113
00:07:53,050 --> 00:07:57,968
In workflow 11 the deploy job does that
with the environment taken from an input.

114
00:07:58,008 --> 00:08:03,049
So an environment secret is read where the
environment is named: in the called job.

115
00:08:03,389 --> 00:08:08,360
env doesn't cross. Variables in the caller's
workflow-level env aren't visible in

116
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:12,072
the called workflow. Use inputs,
or configuration variables.

117
00:08:12,412 --> 00:08:17,132
Permissions only narrow. The called workflow's
token permissions can be reduced, not

118
00:08:17,172 --> 00:08:19,001
raised, relative to the caller's.

119
00:08:19,341 --> 00:08:23,920
The github context is the caller's:
event, ref, commit and github.workflow.

120
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:25,644
Billing is the caller's too.

121
00:08:25,984 --> 00:08:31,240
Limits: up to ten levels of workflows,
and at most 50 unique reusable workflows

122
00:08:31,280 --> 00:08:35,530
called from one workflow file,
since the sixth of November 2025.

123
00:08:35,870 --> 00:08:40,926
And check names. A required status check
for a job in a called workflow is named

124
00:08:40,966 --> 00:08:45,002
with the calling job's name, a
slash, and the called job's name.

125
00:08:45,042 --> 00:08:49,779
With the two course files, the check
is "Staging / Test, build and deploy".

126
00:08:49,819 --> 00:08:54,237
Renaming either job silently breaks
a rule that requires the old name.

127
00:08:54,577 --> 00:08:59,540
One point is unverified, and it's on screen
now. How the secrets of an environment

128
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:04,665
that a called job names appear in that job,
when the caller passes named secrets and

129
00:09:04,705 --> 00:09:09,668
doesn't use secrets: inherit, isn't spelled
out on the pages read for the chapter.

130
00:09:09,708 --> 00:09:14,671
Workflow 11 avoids the question. Verify
the behavior in your repository before you

131
00:09:14,711 --> 00:09:15,464
depend on it.

132
00:09:15,804 --> 00:09:20,603
Re-runs. Re-running all jobs resolves a
reference that's not a commit ID again.

133
00:09:20,643 --> 00:09:25,564
Re-running failed or selected jobs uses
the same commit of the called workflow as

134
00:09:25,604 --> 00:09:30,402
the first attempt. A reusable workflow
referenced as @main can therefore differ

135
00:09:30,442 --> 00:09:32,730
between a first run and a full re-run.

136
00:09:33,070 --> 00:09:37,709
Composite actions. The documented
rules that differ from workflow steps.

137
00:09:37,749 --> 00:09:42,909
Every run step must state its shell. Inputs
are read through the inputs context.

138
00:09:42,949 --> 00:09:47,719
Outputs need a value that maps to a
step output. The secrets context isn't

139
00:09:47,759 --> 00:09:52,918
available. And the parallel-step keywords
of June 2026 can't be used inside one.

140
00:09:52,958 --> 00:09:58,248
In the log the whole action is one step,
which makes a failure inside it harder to

141
00:09:58,288 --> 00:10:00,782
locate than the same steps written out.

142
00:10:01,122 --> 00:10:05,944
Which one. The textbook's rule. Steps that
repeat inside jobs: a composite action.

143
00:10:05,984 --> 00:10:10,569
A whole job, or a job that needs an
environment, a runner choice or secrets: a

144
00:10:10,609 --> 00:10:15,491
reusable workflow. A tool that needs an
operating system environment you don't want

145
00:10:15,531 --> 00:10:20,294
on the runner: a container action. And
neither, when the duplication is two short

146
00:10:20,334 --> 00:10:24,918
blocks in one file: YAML anchors or plain
repetition are easier to read and to

147
00:10:24,958 --> 00:10:25,274
debug.

148
00:10:27,114 --> 00:10:29,410
A picture helps, and this one has trains.

149
00:10:29,750 --> 00:10:34,807
Analogy for concurrency, from the textbook.
A single-track railway section with one

150
00:10:34,847 --> 00:10:39,597
waiting siding. One train is on the track.
A second train waits in the siding.

151
00:10:39,637 --> 00:10:44,203
If a third arrives, the second is sent
away and the third takes the siding.

152
00:10:44,243 --> 00:10:49,362
With cancel-in-progress: true, the arriving
train also removes the one on the track.

153
00:10:49,702 --> 00:10:54,906
The analogy breaks with queue: max, which
turns the siding into a yard for up to 100

154
00:10:54,946 --> 00:10:55,343
trains.

155
00:10:55,683 --> 00:10:58,879
Now run the three merges
from the opening through it.

156
00:10:58,919 --> 00:11:02,359
Merge one is on the track.
Merge two waits in the siding.

157
00:11:02,399 --> 00:11:07,120
Merge three arrives, merge two is sent
away, and merge three takes the siding.

158
00:11:07,160 --> 00:11:11,455
Two deployments, and that's the release
manager's afternoon, explained.

159
00:11:11,495 --> 00:11:16,400
Is anything lost? Usually not, because the
third run's commit contains the second

160
00:11:16,440 --> 00:11:21,528
merge. It's not fine when each run must
happen, for example a database migration per

161
00:11:21,568 --> 00:11:26,351
commit. Then you use queue: max, or you
design the deployment to be cumulative.

162
00:11:26,691 --> 00:11:31,628
Analogy for a reusable workflow. A subcontractor
who brings a whole crew, their own

163
00:11:31,668 --> 00:11:36,544
tools and their own site rules, and works
to a written order, which is the inputs.

164
00:11:36,584 --> 00:11:41,401
You can't give the crew individual
instructions. You can only place the order and

165
00:11:41,441 --> 00:11:44,100
read the delivery note,
which is the outputs.

166
00:11:44,440 --> 00:11:48,735
The textbook says it breaks on trust:
the subcontractor works with your access

167
00:11:48,775 --> 00:11:52,292
badge, and can use less of its
access than you gave, never more.

168
00:11:52,332 --> 00:11:56,516
That is "permissions only narrow", and
"the github context is the caller's".

169
00:11:58,356 --> 00:12:02,473
Try it now, on paper. Thirty
seconds. Copy these three bars.

170
00:12:02,513 --> 00:12:07,530
Then draw them again for cancel-in-progress:
true. Which runs finish now?

171
00:12:09,870 --> 00:12:15,598
Only run 3. Run 1 is cancelled when run
2 arrives, and run 2 when run 3 arrives.

172
00:12:15,938 --> 00:12:19,663
So the default once more: one
running, one pending, one cancelled.

173
00:12:19,703 --> 00:12:24,341
Redraw it in your head for queue: max.
Nothing is cancelled, and the three run one

174
00:12:24,381 --> 00:12:25,140
after another.

175
00:12:26,980 --> 00:12:27,740
Into the lab.

176
00:12:28,080 --> 00:12:35,956
Step 1: what makes groups distinct.
One snippet from the replay labs/run

177
00:12:35,996 --> 00:12:42,772
ch20b/lab-28-1-broken-workflows,
which video 154 uses in full.

178
00:12:43,112 --> 00:12:48,462
Three branches, each of which gets pushes.
A workflow's group is named ci, with

179
00:12:48,502 --> 00:12:54,057
cancel-in-progress: true. A colleague pushes
to docs/rollback-steps while your run

180
00:12:54,097 --> 00:12:58,492
on feature/safety-stock is in
progress. What happens to your run?

181
00:12:58,532 --> 00:12:59,583
Say it out loud.

182
00:13:04,423 --> 00:13:09,088
It's cancelled. A group named ci
gives all three refs one track.

183
00:13:09,128 --> 00:13:12,912
A group that contains
github.ref gives each its own.

184
00:13:12,952 --> 00:13:19,087
Section 20B.16 lists exactly this symptom:
CI runs of different branches cancel each

185
00:13:19,127 --> 00:13:21,955
other, because the group lacks the ref.

186
00:13:22,295 --> 00:13:28,785
Step 2: which version of a reusable
workflow runs. Replay labs/run

187
00:13:28,825 --> 00:13:31,556
ch20b/lab-27-5-reusable-ref.

188
00:13:31,896 --> 00:13:37,119
All four read. The repository has a
caller, deploy.yml, and a called file,

189
00:13:37,159 --> 00:13:43,022
reusable-deploy.yml. A branch, ci/python-version,
adds an input to the called file.

190
00:13:43,362 --> 00:13:48,717
git grep -c counts matches per ref. For
which of the two refs will it print a line?

191
00:13:48,757 --> 00:13:50,082
Make your prediction.

192
00:13:54,922 --> 00:13:56,018
Only for the branch.

193
00:13:56,358 --> 00:14:01,344
The caller on main uses the ./ form, which
means: the called file from the same

194
00:14:01,384 --> 00:14:06,179
commit as the caller. So a branch that
changes the called file tests its own

195
00:14:06,219 --> 00:14:09,869
version, and main keeps using
the old one until the merge.

196
00:14:10,209 --> 00:14:12,947
Step 3: caller and called file disagree.

197
00:14:13,287 --> 00:14:18,060
git commit adds an object and moves the
branch. On main, the caller starts passing

198
00:14:18,100 --> 00:14:18,882
the new input.

199
00:14:19,222 --> 00:14:23,185
Does the called file on main declare
that input? Make your prediction.

200
00:14:28,025 --> 00:14:29,930
Exit status 1: no match.

201
00:14:30,270 --> 00:14:35,143
The caller on main now passes an input that
the called file on main doesn't declare.

202
00:14:35,183 --> 00:14:38,478
You found the disagreement
with git grep, before any run.

203
00:14:38,818 --> 00:14:42,442
The recovery brings the
declaration to main with a merge.

204
00:14:42,482 --> 00:14:47,843
git merge --no-ff is 🟡 CAUTION. Now both
files mention the input in the same commit.

205
00:14:48,183 --> 00:14:52,734
The rule from the textbook: caller and
called file must agree in one commit when

206
00:14:52,774 --> 00:14:56,694
they live in one repository, and
across a pinned ref when they don't.

207
00:14:57,034 --> 00:15:00,891
Lab 27.5 on your practice
repository, in your normal shell.

208
00:15:00,931 --> 00:15:06,372
The interface changes. The lab text and the
linked documentation are the reference.

209
00:15:06,412 --> 00:15:09,343
No GitHub output was
captured by the authors.

210
00:15:09,683 --> 00:15:14,306
Add the two files of workflow 11. After
the run, open it and look at the list of

211
00:15:14,346 --> 00:15:18,969
jobs. Find the job that came from the
called workflow and read its full name: it

212
00:15:19,009 --> 00:15:22,700
should be composed of two names,
as the documentation describes.

213
00:15:22,740 --> 00:15:27,596
Write that name down exactly, because it's
the name a ruleset would have to require.

214
00:15:27,636 --> 00:15:32,317
Then open the summary and find the revision
that the second job wrote: one string

215
00:15:32,357 --> 00:15:36,105
that crossed from the called
workflow to the caller as an output.

216
00:15:36,445 --> 00:15:41,194
Then the lab's failure case: change the
called file on a branch and open a pull

217
00:15:41,234 --> 00:15:46,104
request. Ask yourself before you look:
which version of the called file does this

218
00:15:46,144 --> 00:15:47,498
pull request's run use?

219
00:15:49,338 --> 00:15:50,986
Five mistakes to watch for.

220
00:15:51,326 --> 00:15:56,644
A CI group without the ref. Root cause: a
group is a name shared by everything that

221
00:15:56,684 --> 00:16:00,324
uses it, so runs of different
branches cancel each other.

222
00:16:00,664 --> 00:16:05,881
Expecting every queued deployment to run.
Root cause: by default a newly queued run

223
00:16:05,921 --> 00:16:08,731
cancels the run already
pending in the group.

224
00:16:09,071 --> 00:16:13,715
cancel-in-progress: true on a deployment.
Root cause: the run in progress is

225
00:16:13,755 --> 00:16:17,044
cancelled half-way and leaves
a state nobody designed.

226
00:16:17,384 --> 00:16:22,368
An empty secret in a called workflow. Root
cause: secrets pass one level only, and

227
00:16:22,408 --> 00:16:27,271
an environment secret cannot be passed by
the caller; it is read in the job that

228
00:16:27,311 --> 00:16:28,619
names the environment.

229
00:16:28,959 --> 00:16:32,109
Renaming a job after extracting
a reusable workflow.

230
00:16:32,149 --> 00:16:37,079
Root cause: the check is named from the
calling job and the called job, so a rule

231
00:16:37,119 --> 00:16:41,189
that requires the old name waits
for a check that no longer exists.

232
00:16:43,029 --> 00:16:48,113
Now, out of the lab. The textbook's case:
a platform team owns one deploy workflow

233
00:16:48,153 --> 00:16:53,237
in a central repository. Forty service
repositories call it at a pinned commit and

234
00:16:53,277 --> 00:16:58,423
pass an environment name. A change to the
deployment procedure is one reviewed pull

235
00:16:58,463 --> 00:17:01,734
request, rolled out by
Dependabot updates of the pin.

236
00:17:02,074 --> 00:17:06,638
Then the sentence that makes it a security
design and not only a convenience: that

237
00:17:06,678 --> 00:17:11,242
one file runs with deployment credentials
in forty repositories, so its repository

238
00:17:11,282 --> 00:17:13,937
needs the strictest ruleset
in the organization.

239
00:17:14,277 --> 00:17:18,270
And the trap the textbook attaches
to concurrency in this setting.

240
00:17:18,310 --> 00:17:21,936
A called workflow sees the
caller's name in github.workflow.

241
00:17:21,976 --> 00:17:27,068
So if the caller and the called workflow
both use the group built from workflow name

242
00:17:27,108 --> 00:17:31,834
and ref, with cancel-in-progress: true,
the called job cancels its own caller.

243
00:17:32,174 --> 00:17:37,446
One counterweight from section 20B.17:
don't extract a reusable workflow for two

244
00:17:37,486 --> 00:17:42,958
callers. Indirection costs debugging time,
and the check name changes and can break

245
00:17:42,998 --> 00:17:44,020
required checks.

246
00:17:45,860 --> 00:17:52,985
Your turn. Do Lab 27.5, "A reusable
workflow and its caller (workflow 11)", in

247
00:17:53,025 --> 00:17:56,660
lab-manual/m27-build-package-deliver.md.

248
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:01,585
Before the first run, predict the exact
name of the check that the called job will

249
00:18:01,625 --> 00:18:06,211
report. Before the failure case, predict
which version of the called file the pull

250
00:18:06,251 --> 00:18:07,283
request's run uses.

251
00:18:07,623 --> 00:18:14,239
The challenge is Exercise 27.5, "Four
reusable-workflow surprises", in

252
00:18:14,279 --> 00:18:17,853
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

253
00:18:21,693 --> 00:18:26,362
Answer out loud. A strong answer
states the default rule first, in the

254
00:18:26,402 --> 00:18:31,945
documentation's terms: in progress, pending,
and what happens to a pending run when

255
00:18:31,985 --> 00:18:37,596
another is queued. Then it walks the three
runs through that rule on a timeline, and

256
00:18:37,636 --> 00:18:42,304
says whether anything was lost and
under which condition that matters.

257
00:18:42,344 --> 00:18:46,610
The follow-up combines queue: max
with cancel-in-progress: true.

258
00:18:46,650 --> 00:18:52,193
Say what GitHub does with that combination,
and then judge each of the two settings

259
00:18:52,233 --> 00:18:54,076
separately for a deployment.

260
00:18:55,916 --> 00:18:56,844
Let's land this.

261
00:18:57,184 --> 00:18:58,843
You should now be able to say:

262
00:18:59,183 --> 00:19:04,254
A concurrency group allows one run in progress
and, by default, one pending; a newly

263
00:19:04,294 --> 00:19:06,869
queued run cancels the
one already pending.

264
00:19:07,209 --> 00:19:12,183
CI wants a group with workflow name and ref
and cancellation; a deployment wants one

265
00:19:12,223 --> 00:19:14,093
fixed group and no cancellation.

266
00:19:14,433 --> 00:19:18,505
A reusable workflow replaces whole jobs,
a composite action replaces steps, a

267
00:19:18,545 --> 00:19:21,282
container action replaces
one step in its own image.

268
00:19:21,622 --> 00:19:26,055
A called job's check is named from the
calling job and the called job, and an

269
00:19:26,095 --> 00:19:30,238
environment secret is read in the
called job that names the environment.

270
00:19:30,578 --> 00:19:35,399
With the ./ form the called file comes
from the same commit as the caller; across

271
00:19:35,439 --> 00:19:40,020
repositories the ref after the at sign
decides, and it should be a commit ID.

272
00:19:41,860 --> 00:19:50,464
Read sections 20B.5 and 20B.6 of Chapter
20B. Do Exercise 27.3, "Three pushes in ten

273
00:19:50,504 --> 00:19:55,712
minutes", in
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

274
00:19:56,052 --> 00:20:00,408
Three merges, two deployments, and you
can now say why without opening a log.

275
00:20:00,448 --> 00:20:04,062
Draw the three bars once more from
memory before the next video.

276
00:20:04,102 --> 00:20:08,344
Next time: publishing a container image,
and release automation in outline.

277
00:20:08,384 --> 00:20:11,313
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

278
00:20:11,353 --> 00:20:12,684
See you in the next one.
